 it was about 32 degrees celsius  at the time of the accident there is an  ongoing investigation with bnsf amtrak  and  ntsb we ask that people stay clear of  the area until the investigation is  complete as far as hospitals  most people were treated and released  amtrak has had a handful of accidents  but hasn't had a train derail since  december 2017. that train was traveling  at more than twice the speed limit in  washington state sending passenger cars  tumbling from a bridge killing three  people many travelers on this train were  taken to a nearby school thankful to be  alive i was laying down in my in my  compartment on a bed  so luckily i just tumbled just in the  room i heard a loud bang and then the  cars tipped the train was carrying more  than 140 passengers and 16 crew members  the ntsb has no timetable for releasing  its findings its report could take  months jennifer johnson global news  washington  imagine finding john lennon's voice in  your house we'll explain when global  national continues  you're watching global national  [Music]  does this sound familiar john lennon and  paul mccartney are topping music charts  again with a hip-hop take on their song  michelle canadian rapper drake listed  the two beatles as co-writers on his  number four hit champagne poetry  the band remains one of the most  influential in history in newly  discovered interviews going as far back  as 1969 lennon talks about what  influenced the beatles a canadian family  found the rare tapes which go on auction  this week as crystal gomezing reports  they're a priceless piece of music  history  [Music]  what beetle music do you look upon as  being outstanding  well i'm prejudiced i like my own you  know  and so i don't know you know i like  revolution number nine one voice a music  legend the other a canadian journalist  from winnipeg  name other favorites from the 60s walrus  strawberry fields day in the life  why walrus for instance because i wrote  it and i like it you know  the late ken zielig's multiple  interviews with john lennon and yoko ono  were discovered this past summer a  beatles treasure hidden away in los  angeles in the garage of his youngest  daughter the family has chosen to put  the reels all 91 minutes up for auction  interesting topics that he covers are  the you know the songs that he was  he likes the beatles songs that he likes  his relationship with the beatles  his his love for yoko and their  relationship the beatles are a cultural  touchstone the abbey road crosswalk is  proof of that it's one of the most  pictured locations in london hannah  zelik the eldest daughter didn't want to  go on camera but she did tell me sitting  and listening to her dad's recordings  made it feel like john lennon was in her  home speaking directly to her an  experience up for grabs comes september  28th  music writer and broadcaster alan cross  says demand for beatles memorabilia is  endless and these recordings  are rare the idea of being the owner of  a number of interviews that have never  been released to anybody up until this  time that's a pretty good bit of  memorabilia john could you give me some  idea what the beatles plan to do in the  future  well the beatles don't usually plan  their future you know we play everything  by ear  let me take you down  gold dust unearthed during a pandemic  cleaning spree  crystal gomanson global news  london  up next an artist honors the lost  children of residential  schools the pain is still fresh for the  more than 200 unmarked graves found at  the former residential school in  kamloops bc now there are plans to carve  a permanent monument in memory of the  indigenous children who have yet to be  identified julia foy spoke to the artist  tasked with the tribute and the vision  that's inspiring him and a warning some  of the details may be distressing  when i first looked at it i saw  this would be the owl's head  and having its wing extended here darcy  basil is about to transform this 1100  kilogram jade boulder into a tribute to  the lost children of the kamloops  residential school  my dad went to the campus residential  school and so  he told me these stories that's why it  was so hard for me  i wasn't able to work the vancouver  artist wanted to find a way to help  survivors heal it wasn't until he was  offered the jade from the yukon that the  inspiration came to him in our culture  the owl represents death but  on the other side of it thou represents  taking your spirit and guiding it to the  spirit world he traveled to kamloops to  consult with the tekamloops sequem the  band had a similar vision they  photocopied a story about  the owl  taking the child because he didn't  listen  and brought him away and raised him  basil had a third confirmation after an  encounter with a bird i went for a walk  in the trails  and  owl flew by me about a foot in front of  my face and  turned and dropped a feather for me  several carvers and mentors will cheer  basil on during the next year and he's  up there now i was surprised how fast  he's progressed yeah it's been it's been  a nice journey he hopes the project  could lead to an indigenous carving  center for artists from around the world  it could turn into a  mentorship program it could turn into a  school it could turn into just about  anything yeah this is what it looks like  in its natural state the project will  begin this week coinciding with the  first national day for truth and  reconciliation i think the jade piece is  something that will last for thousands  of years and it's strong  it's resilient  and it's beautiful and just like the  ceramic people his dream is about to  take flight  julia foy global news vancouver  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm robin gill tonight your  canada is the pitt river in british  columbia we'd love to see your corner of  the country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna friesen  will be back at the anchor desk tomorrow  i'll see you next weekend have a great  night  [Music]  you
 

